---
title: "Tutorial 2: Convert a NAHPU Data Package to the LSUMZ Specify sheet"
sidebar:
  label: "2. Convert a Data Package with R or Python"
  order: 2
---

[Tutorial 1](/en/tutorials/export/specify-export-preset/) builds the LSUMZ Mammalogy
WorkBench sheet inside NAHPU with an export preset. This tutorial produces the
same sheet outside the app: you export a
[NAHPU Data Package](/en/usages/export/export-bundles/#nahpu-data-package) and
convert it with a ready-made script in Python or R.

Use this route when:

- the data needs checking or cleaning in a script before it goes to Specify;
- someone without NAHPU on their device prepares the upload;
- you want a reproducible, versioned record of how the sheet was made.

Both scripts live in the
[lsumz-mammal-specify-frictionless](https://github.com/nahpu/lsumz-mammal-specify-frictionless)
repository. They read the package with the
[Frictionless](https://frictionlessdata.io) library for each language and write
identical CSV files.

## Before you start

You need:

- a NAHPU project with mammal specimen records;
- uv, Python 3.10 or newer with pip, or R with permission to install packages;
- a copy of the repository, cloned with Git or downloaded as a ZIP from GitHub.

[Set up your computer](#set-up-your-computer) covers each of these.

The repository is organized like this:

{% filetree %}
- data/
  - country-continent.csv
  - example/
    - ambang-shrews.nahpu-dp.zip
    - expected-specify.csv
- python/
  - nahpu_to_specify.py
  - tests/
- R/
  - nahpu_to_specify.R
  - tests/
- results/
{% /filetree %}

## Set up your computer

Do this once per computer. The commands run in a terminal: Terminal on macOS,
PowerShell on Windows, or your distribution's terminal on Linux.

### Get the repository

With [Git](https://git-scm.com/downloads):

```bash
git clone https://github.com/nahpu/lsumz-mammal-specify-frictionless.git
cd lsumz-mammal-specify-frictionless
```

Without Git, open the repository on GitHub, select `Code`, then
`Download ZIP`, extract the archive, and open a terminal in the extracted
folder.

### Install the tools

Choose uv or pip for Python, or install R. uv is the simpler route: it
installs a suitable Python version and the script's packages for you.

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="uv" %}

1. Install uv.

   On macOS or Linux:

   ```bash
   curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh
   ```

   On Windows, in PowerShell:

   ```powershell
   powershell -ExecutionPolicy ByPass -c "irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex"
   ```

   With a package manager, use `brew install uv` on macOS or
   `winget install --id=astral-sh.uv -e` on Windows.

2. Close and reopen the terminal, then check the installation:

   ```bash
   uv --version
   ```

3. From the repository folder, install the script's dependencies:

   ```bash
   cd python
   uv sync
   ```

   uv downloads Python 3.10 or newer if your computer does not have it and
   creates a virtual environment in `python/.venv`. Prefix Python commands
   with `uv run` to use it; you do not need to activate it.

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="pip" %}

1. Install Python 3.10 or newer.

   - **Windows:** download the installer from
     [python.org](https://www.python.org/downloads/) and select
     `Add python.exe to PATH` on its first screen.
   - **macOS:** download the installer from
     [python.org](https://www.python.org/downloads/), or run
     `brew install python`.
   - **Linux:** install Python with your package manager, for example
     `sudo apt install python3 python3-venv python3-pip` on Debian or Ubuntu.

2. Check the installation. Use `python3` on macOS and Linux and `py` on
   Windows:

   ```bash
   python3 --version
   ```

3. From the repository folder, create a virtual environment so the packages
   stay separate from other Python projects:

   ```bash
   python3 -m venv .venv
   ```

   On Windows, run `py -m venv .venv`.

4. Activate the environment.

   On macOS or Linux:

   ```bash
   source .venv/bin/activate
   ```

   On Windows, in PowerShell:

   ```powershell
   .venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
   ```

   The prompt now starts with `(.venv)`, and `python` points to the
   environment on every platform. Activate it again each time you open a new
   terminal.

5. Install the script's dependency:

   ```bash
   python -m pip install frictionless
   ```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="R" %}

1. Install R from [CRAN](https://cran.r-project.org/). An editor such as
   [RStudio](https://posit.co/download/rstudio-desktop/) is optional.
2. Check that `Rscript` runs from a terminal:

   ```bash
   Rscript --version
   ```

   On Windows, if the command is not found, add R's `bin` folder, such as
   `C:\Program Files\R\R-<version>\bin`, to your `PATH`, or run the commands from
   an R session instead.

3. Install the packages listed in [step 2](#2-try-the-bundled-example).

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

## 1. Export the NAHPU Data Package

{% steps %}

1. Open the project menu and select `Bundle records`.
2. Set `Bundle format` to `NAHPU Data Package`.
3. Keep `TAR.GZ` or choose `ZIP` as the `Archive format`. The scripts read
   both.
4. Name the file, choose a folder, and select `Create bundle`.
5. Copy the file, such as `ambang.nahpu-dp.tar.gz`, into the repository's
   `data/` folder.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="caution" %}
A NAHPU Data Package holds the whole project, including personnel contact
details and user settings. Keep it out of public repositories; commit only the
converted sheet if the data may be shared.
{% /aside %}

## 2. Try the bundled example

Run a converter once without arguments. It converts
`data/example/ambang-shrews.nahpu-dp.zip` and writes
`results/ambang-shrews-specify.csv`, which should match
`data/example/expected-specify.csv`, five rows of the real LSUMZ incoming
sheet.

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="Python" %}

With uv, from the `python/` folder:

```bash
uv run python nahpu_to_specify.py
```

With pip, from the repository folder with `.venv` activated:

```bash
python python/nahpu_to_specify.py
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="R" %}

Install the packages once from an R session:

```r
install.packages(c(
  "frictionless", "here", "dplyr", "tidyr", "readr", "purrr", "stringr",
  "lubridate", "testthat"
))
```

Then run from the repository root:

```bash
Rscript R/nahpu_to_specify.R
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

If the result matches the expected file, your setup works.

## 3. Convert your package

Pass your package as the first argument. The sheet is written to `results/`
under the package name.

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="Python" %}

With uv, from the `python/` folder:

```bash
uv run python nahpu_to_specify.py ../data/ambang.nahpu-dp.tar.gz
```

With pip, from the repository folder with `.venv` activated:

```bash
python python/nahpu_to_specify.py data/ambang.nahpu-dp.tar.gz
```

Add `-o PATH` to choose the output file, and `--all-taxa` to keep specimens
outside the mammal taxon groups.

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="R" %}

```bash
Rscript R/nahpu_to_specify.R data/ambang.nahpu-dp.tar.gz
```

A second argument sets the output file, and `--all-taxa` keeps specimens
outside the mammal taxon groups. In an R session, source the script and call the
function instead:

```r
source(here::here("R", "nahpu_to_specify.R"))
sheet <- nahpu_to_specify(
  here::here("data", "ambang.nahpu-dp.tar.gz"),
  here::here("results", "ambang-specify.csv")
)
```

`sheet` is a tibble, so you can check it with dplyr before uploading.

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

The scripts also accept an extracted package folder or its `datapackage.json`.

## 4. Check the sheet

Open the CSV and compare a few specimens with their NAHPU records:

- `Sex` reads `Male` or `Female`. The scripts decode the stored code with the
  package's `mappings/sqlite_enums.csv`.
- `Continent` is filled from `Country`. A country missing from
  `data/country-continent.csv` leaves it blank and prints a warning; add the
  country to that file and run again.
- `Collector1LastName` reads `Last, First`. The last word of the cataloger's
  name is moved to the front, so check two-word family names.
- `Start Date` is the specimen's collection date as `dd/mm/yyyy`.
- Each part fills one `PrepType` group, with its storage location, such as a
  freezer box position, in `PrepType`*n*`Location`. `Remarks` follows the
  second group, as on the LSUMZ sheet.
- A measurement in brackets, such as `[150]`, means its accuracy note marks it
  as unreliable, the same rule the `LSUMZ-mammalogy` preset from
  [Tutorial 1](/en/tutorials/export/specify-export-preset/) uses.

## 5. Upload to Specify

Import the CSV as a new WorkBench data set, apply the LSUMZ Mammalogy upload
plan, validate, and upload, as in
[Tutorial 1](/en/tutorials/export/specify-export-preset/#8-export-and-import-into-specify).
The columns are the same, so the same upload plan works for sheets from either
route.

## How the conversion works

Each script follows the package's relational tables:

1. It keeps specimens whose `taxonGroup` is Mammals, General Mammals, or Bats.
2. It joins `taxonomy` through `speciesID`, `mammalAttribute` through the
   specimen UUID, and the cataloger in `personnel`.
3. It finds the locality through the collecting event's site and its
   `geography` row.
4. It uses the specimen's own coordinate, or the site's coordinate when the site
   has exactly one, as NAHPU does.
5. It spreads `specimenPart` rows into numbered `PrepType` columns in part
   order.

Empty NAHPU tables are left out of a Data Package, so the scripts treat a
missing table as empty rather than failing. To adapt the scripts to another
collection's sheet, change the column lists at the top of either file. For a
different layout altogether, write your own script as in
[Tutorial 3](/en/tutorials/export/custom-data-package-script/).

